  "id": "Compound::DB00320 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Dihydroergotamine is linked to Cyp3a4 through a series of molecular interactions involving ergonovine and hydralazine, which are both bound to the enzyme. The evidence suggests that Dihydroergotamine may act as a causal agent in the development of conditions like arteriospasm, oesophageal spasm, and retroperitoneal fibrosis, all of which are associated with ergonovine. Ergonovine, in turn, binds to Cyp3a4, indicating a potential mechanism by which Dihydroergotamine indirectly influences Cyp3a4 activity. The shared ancestry of Dihydroergotamine and ergonovine as dopamine agonists and ergot alkaloids provides a plausible biological basis for this relationship. The evidence supports a pathway where Dihydroergotamine may trigger a cascade leading to ergonovine release, which then binds to Cyp3a4, suggesting a possible role in the regulation of cytochrome P450 enzyme activity. This mechanism highlights the importance of ergonovine in mediating the interaction between Dihydroergotamine and Cyp3a4.",
